plato-gorgias-3.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: plato-gorgias-3.logic.r1 has "indulge → natural_justice"; plato-gorgias-3.logic.r1.2 and plato-gorgias-3.logic.r1.3 have "natural_justice"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
plato-gorgias-3.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps plato-gorgias-3.logic.r1.2 and plato-gorgias-3.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "one allows his desires to wax to the uttermost and, having courage and intelligence, ministers to them and satisfies all his longings" and "this is natural justice and nobility" are false. Is S3, "And this I affirm to be natural justice and nobility.", true then? [in 1 of 2 runs]
plato-gorgias-3.md:1: argue/readings: S6 is read 2 ways: plato-gorgias-3.logic.r1 has "capable → temperance_base" and "capable ∧ temperate → servant ∧ miserable"; plato-gorgias-3.logic.r1.2 and plato-gorgias-3.logic.r1.3 have "capable → temperance_base" and "capable ∧ temperate → miserable"; they differ when only "a man is the son of a king or has a nature capable of acquiring an empire, a tyranny or sovereignty", "temperance is base and evil for such a man", "the man admits custom, reason and the opinion of other men to be lords over him" and "the man is in a miserable plight" are true, which the second allows
plato-gorgias-3.md:1: argue/answered: S6: answered yes, which keeps plato-gorgias-3.logic.r1.2 and plato-gorgias-3.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "a man is the son of a king or has a nature capable of acquiring an empire, a tyranny or sovereignty", "temperance is base and evil for such a man", "the man admits custom, reason and the opinion of other men to be lords over him" and "the man is in a miserable plight" are true and "a man is the servant of anything" is false. Is S6, "For if a man had been originally the son of a king, or had a nature capable of acquiring an empire or a tyranny or sovereignty, what could be more truly base or evil than temperance—to a man like him, I say, who might freely be enjoying every good, and has no one to stand in his way, and yet has admitted custom and reason and the opinion of other men to be lords over him?—must not he be in a miserable plight whom the reputation of justice and temperance hinders from giving more to his friends than to his enemies, even though he be a ruler in his city?", true then? [in 1 of 2 runs]
plato-gorgias-3.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: plato-gorgias-3.logic.r1 has "natural_justice → virtue"; plato-gorgias-3.logic.r1.2 and plato-gorgias-3.logic.r1.3 have nothing from it; they differ when only "this is natural justice and nobility" is true, which the second allows
plato-gorgias-3.logic.r1: argue/readings: U1: plato-gorgias-3.logic.r1 takes for granted "if this is natural justice and nobility, then luxury, intemperance and licence are virtue"; plato-gorgias-3.logic.r1.2 and plato-gorgias-3.logic.r1.3 do not
plato-gorgias-3.logic.r1: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps plato-gorgias-3.logic.r1, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if this is natural justice and nobility, then luxury, intemperance and licence are virtue"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
plato-gorgias-3.md:1: argue/unfaithful: plato-gorgias-3.logic.r1 P3 (S3): the sentence has no word of "one allows his desires to wax to the uttermost and, having courage and intelligence, ministers to them and satisfies all his longings": is it the sentence P3 came from?
plato-gorgias-3.md:1: argue/negation: plato-gorgias-3.logic.r1 P6 (S6): the sentence says "no", but P6 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "if a man is the son of a king or has a nature capable of acquiring an empire, a tyranny or sovereignty, then temperance is base and evil for such a man"
plato-gorgias-3.md:1: argue/negation: plato-gorgias-3.logic.r1 P7 (S6): the sentence says "no", but P7 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "if (a man is the son of a king or has a nature capable of acquiring an empire, a tyranny or sovereignty and the man admits custom, reason and the opinion of other men to be lords over him), then (a man is the servant of anything and the man is in a miserable plight)"
plato-gorgias-3.md:1: argue/negation: plato-gorgias-3.logic.r1 C (S7): the sentence says "nothing", but C denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "if (luxury, intemperance and licence and they are provided with means), then (luxury, intemperance and licence are virtue and a man is happy)"
